#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a990e253-b3a4-5d59-b6e5-c06d24c3b06a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266920} {:answer/id #uuid "cf405392-15e5-5454-af1a-280b01969c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266921} {:answer/id #uuid "9b109a4d-c456-5841-b777-442d36055c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266922} {:answer/id #uuid "9d396a8c-0a15-5ddb-af03-bb2c4cead9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266923} {:answer/id #uuid "89ad5c01-b932-56ae-8068-44b86976256a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b109a4d-c456-5841-b777-442d36055c46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266922} :answer-field/id #uuid "67b49cf0-1d85-5436-ab3f-6617d0b013f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266919}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f402bba5-eaa5-542e-ba47-73c83d9ce48d" :question/math-academy-id "q-12018" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 6(2x-3)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-3.$Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6(2x-3)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{u}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}){u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-{u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(2x-3)^{-3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-(-3)\\cdot (2x-3)^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-3)+0 \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2x-3)^{-4}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61bbdade-e0e6-5726-b5b4-d780cd95fef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{(1-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266927} {:answer/id #uuid "a733e1d0-caf5-53a4-b41e-ac894295a0f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266928} {:answer/id #uuid "335d4a4f-8a5e-5e3e-b000-15aea73bd85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266929} {:answer/id #uuid "82034522-e066-5cba-902c-11a76a304253" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266930} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee381b6-74cc-5933-bbce-4f3a2c84d262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{(1-9z)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "335d4a4f-8a5e-5e3e-b000-15aea73bd85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266929} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e45116f-a3fa-59bc-b604-14c6ecf24e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266926}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78d0396e-82eb-542b-a43f-b6df87f217ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-49431" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9z}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=1-9z.$ Then differentiating with respect to $z$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=-9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}z=-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\frac{1}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(1-9z)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{1-9z}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[\\sqrt{1-9z}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[(1-9z)^{1/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(1-9z)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =(1-9z)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9z}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69ef12d7-402a-50fb-b096-c48bd22ccc9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266934} {:answer/id #uuid "1f6d28a2-78f9-5a9b-8b12-757937c5b95d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266935} {:answer/id #uuid "38f74021-d5fb-5bf5-a0b0-33a371ed29f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266936} {:answer/id #uuid "7c899c38-cbe7-534f-861a-a14f320c2c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266937} {:answer/id #uuid "544473ea-e71e-5508-ad79-16c54f6b5568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ef12d7-402a-50fb-b096-c48bd22ccc9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266934} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7619ece-38ea-59d2-a313-65f7b590f0ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266933}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7f9bd93-5375-50fc-95cf-f71de7f34eef" :question/math-academy-id "q-49437" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (y+11)^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=y+11.$ Then differentiating with respect to $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}y=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (y+11)^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {u}^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{8/3}}{8/3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(y+11)^{8/3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}] & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[(y+11)^{8/3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}(y+11)^{5/3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+11)+0 \\\\  & =(y+11)^{5/3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{5}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff0f7e33-76e7-5e56-978c-f942b65e6a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266941} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1775d3-358d-5b89-b0d3-396a700f8e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266942} {:answer/id #uuid "8df04daa-221c-5ae1-b949-9dbef49ab348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266943} {:answer/id #uuid "5d83df84-2574-5210-a7f2-2bda41daf14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266944} {:answer/id #uuid "7725a6f2-3735-5fd8-b9b7-ab6aa07a304b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7725a6f2-3735-5fd8-b9b7-ab6aa07a304b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266945} :answer-field/id #uuid "30357bab-78f2-55e1-8f33-6fcf7e0bb188" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266940}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2058ea27-d95b-5f37-8b22-5c724bfe6f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49468" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}s=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int {(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=s+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}s}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}s=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}s & =\\int {u}^{-3/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2/5}{u}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{u}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{(s+1)}^{2/5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[{(s+1)}^{2/5}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s+1)+0 \\\\  & ={(s+1)}^{-3/5}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{(s+1)}^{3}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387d85df-b839-5837-89b9-5e4bb4daa073" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Negative Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0bcbeb-6bad-5455-80d0-9e4abbcc2df4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266948} {:answer/id #uuid "49641b55-437c-50ae-8f9a-753a66d04084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266949} {:answer/id #uuid "6755725f-7aed-5c17-a2bf-fcfb68304cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266950} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a509eb-a4d5-5298-b485-63a3d5563538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266951} {:answer/id #uuid "9e033727-9c32-57f8-b869-e09491691fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e033727-9c32-57f8-b869-e09491691fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266952} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc21d131-2d3a-5323-b643-13e942c8a711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266947}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3764bbdf-f69f-58d6-a98a-c5240e17e994" :question/math-academy-id "q-72429" :question/problem "$\\int -\\frac{15}{(5x-7)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int -15(5x-7)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x-7.$Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -15(5x-7)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -15{u}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\int {u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}){u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(5x-7)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x-7)^{-3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (5x-7)^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-7)+0 \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (5x-7)^{-4}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{(5x-7)^{4}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
